sect1.sect2:
Binary JDK Information
Creating a JVM from source requires a set of circular dependencies. The first thing that's needed is a set of programs called a Java Development Kit (JDK). This set of programs includes java, javac, jar, and several others. It also includes several base jar files.
To start, we set up a binary installation of the JDK created by the BLFS editors. It is installed in the /opt directory to allow for multiple installations, including a source based version.
&lfs131_checked;
Binary Package Information
Binary download (x86_64):
Download MD5 sum: &javabin-bin-md5sum-x64;
Download size (binary): &javabin-bin-size-x64;
Estimated disk space required: &javabin-bin-buildsize-x64;
Starting with OpenJDK-25, the support for the 32-bit server and client JVM has been removed. OpenJDK can still be built for the zero JVM variant, which does not use any assembly code and has performance issues. The BLFS developers have therefore decided not to provide the 32-bit binary build anymore. If 32-bit is needed, you should use OpenJDK-21, which is well maintained. A java binary can be found at and used to build the most recent version of OpenJDK-21. This binary may be out of date and suffer vulnerabilities, but is good enough to build from source.
